<h2>The question of survival</h2>
<p>Cheque complaints are creatures of the payee: Section 142 of the NI Act requires the complaint to be made by the payee or holder in due course. When a bank assigns a loan book to an ARC midway through a prosecution, accused persons routinely argue that the complaint dies with the assignment &mdash; the ARC was never the payee, so it cannot prosecute. The petitioners pressed exactly this, relying on decisions under the Companies Act and the IBC. The Court held those statutes operate in different fields and cannot control the SARFAESI scheme.</p>

<h2>&ldquo;Other proceeding of whatever nature&rdquo;</h2>
<p>The pivot of the judgment is Section 5(4) of the SARFAESI Act. The Court refused to read the residual phrase narrowly:</p>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>No ejusdem generis.</strong> The words &ldquo;other proceeding of whatever nature&rdquo; are deliberately wide; the principle of ejusdem generis has no application, so the phrase is not shrunk to civil suits and appeals.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Form is not the test.</strong> A Section 138 complaint is criminal in form, but it relates to recovery on a dishonoured cheque forming part of the acquired financial asset. Its criminal character does not, by itself, exclude it from Section 5(4).</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Consent matters.</strong> Section 5(5) lets the ARC seek substitution in pending proceedings &mdash; but with the consent of the originator that filed them.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Strict proof at trial.</strong> The assignment does not make the ARC the payee or holder in due course; that status must be proved on evidence, and the ARC cannot improve upon the original complainant&rsquo;s case.</div>
</div>

<h2>The caveat that defence counsel will use</h2>
<p>The judgment is a win for ARCs at the threshold, but it hands the defence a structured trial strategy. The Court listed what the substituted complainant must establish: that the dishonoured cheque itself travelled with the financial asset under the assignment agreement; that the transfer amounts to an endorsement or assignment sufficient under Sections 8 and 9 of the NI Act; and that every ingredient of Section 138 &mdash; presentment, dishonour, notice, non-payment &mdash; stands proved. As the Court put it, such questions of fact &ldquo;must obviously be decided on the basis of evidence led at the trial.&rdquo;</p>

<h2>Practical notes for both sides</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>For assignees.</strong> The assignment agreement should expressly list the cheques, return memos, statutory notices and pending complaints. Gaps in the schedule become trial-stage vulnerabilities.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>For accused persons.</strong> A threshold challenge to substitution is now largely closed; the contest shifts to the holder-in-due-course question and the Section 138 ingredients, which remain fully open.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Under the new code.</strong> Section 138 NI Act prosecutions instituted after 1 July 2024 follow BNSS procedure; the summary-trial framework and compounding avenues remain the practical routes to resolution.</div>
</div>
<p>For Delhi practitioners, the decision matters daily: a large share of pending Section 138 dockets in the district courts trace back to assigned bank debts, and magistrates now have clear appellate backing both for allowing substitution and for insisting on strict proof thereafter.</p>
<div class="note">This article reports a judgment of the Supreme Court of India for general information only. It is not legal advice and does not comment on any pending proceeding.</div>
